1.1.text:
Article 64

1. Families and communities are obliged to prevent the occurrence of violence and the occurrence of criminal acts against women and children.
2. Every person who hears, sees, or knows of the occurrence of violence is obliged to make efforts in accordance with their capabilities to:a. prevent the continuation of criminal acts;
b. provide protection to victims;
c. provide emergency assistance; and - 37 -d. assist in the process of submitting applications for protection orders.

Article 63

1. To implement the provisions as referred to in Article 62, the Regional Government:a. formulates policies on efforts to prevent violence and prevent criminal acts of trafficking in persons against women and children;
b. formulates policies on the elimination of violence;

36 -
c. conducts communication, information, education, socialization, and advocacy on violence against women and children as well as criminal acts of trafficking in persons against women and children;
d. establishes institutions as a forum for cooperation in the protection and recovery of victims;
e. implements mechanisms for medical services, medicolegal services, psychosocial services, legal services, assistance services, spiritual guidance services, and victim recovery services;
f. provides infrastructure and facilities for the protection and recovery of victims;
g. facilitates the resocialization of victims, so that victims can resume their social functions in society; andh. conducts health rehabilitation services, social rehabilitation, repatriation, and social reintegration for victims of criminal acts of trafficking in persons against women and children.
2. Further provisions regarding the forms and mechanisms of victim protection as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 10

Every child has the right:
a. to survival, growth, development and participation in a reasonable manner in accordance with human dignity and to receive protection from violence and discrimination;
b. to a name as personal identity and citizenship status;
c. to worship according to his/her religion, to think, and to express in accordance with the level of intelligence and age, under parental guidance;
d. to know his/her parents, to be raised, and cared for by his/her own parents;
e. to obtain health services and social security in accordance with physical, mental, spiritual, and social needs; f. to obtain education and teaching in the context of developing his/her personality and level of intelligence in accordance with interests and talents;
g. to express and be heard his/her opinion, to receive, seek, and provide information in accordance with the level of intelligence and age for self-development in accordance with moral values and propriety;
h. to rest and utilize leisure time, to associate with peers, to play, to recreate, and to create in accordance with interests, talents, and level of intelligence for self-development;
i. to receive protection from discriminatory treatment, exploitation, whether economic or sexual, neglect, violence, abuse, injustice, other mistreatment, while in the care of parents, guardians, or any other party responsible for care;

17 -
j. to be cared for by his/her own parents, unless there are reasons and/or valid legal rules showing that the separation is in the best interests of the child and is a last resort; andk. to obtain protection from abuse in political activities, involvement in armed conflicts, involvement in social unrest, involvement in events containing elements of violence and involvement in warfare.

Article 3

The objectives of implementing protection of women are: a. to ensure the fulfillment of women's rights;
b. to protect women and prevent all forms of violence and discrimination against women; andc. to maintain the integrity of a harmonious and prosperous household.

Article 8

1. To implement the provisions as referred to in Article 7, the Regional Government shall organize gender mainstreaming including:a. establishing the implementation of gender mainstreaming;
b. coordinating, facilitating, and mediating gender mainstreaming;
c. facilitating institutional strengthening and development of gender mainstreaming mechanisms in government institutions, women's study centers, research and development institutions, non-governmental institutions;
d. implementing gender mainstreaming related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;
e. improving the quality of life of women related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;
f. integrating efforts to improve the quality of life of women related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;
g. coordinating the implementation of improving the quality of life of women related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;
h. organizing the protection of women especially protection of victims of violence, female workers, elderly women, and women with disabilities, in conflict areas and disaster-affected areas;

15 -
i. facilitating the integration of protection of women especially protection of victims of violence, female workers, elderly women and women with disabilities, in conflict areas and disaster-affected areas;
j. coordinating the protection of women especially protection of victims of violence, female workers, elderly women and women with disabilities;
k. facilitating the strengthening and development of networks of community institutions or organizations and the business sector for the implementation of gender mainstreaming;
l. developing gender-based information systems; andm. providing and organizing integrated or non-integrated services for women victims of violence, female workers, elderly women, and women with disabilities.
2. Further provisions regarding forms of protection of women and gender mainstreaming mechanisms as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 59

Every victim has the right to:
a. obtain protection from the family, Regional Government, police, prosecutor's office, court, advocates, social institutions or other parties either temporarily or based on a protection order from the Court;
b. obtain health services in accordance with medical needs;
c. obtain special handling related to victim confidentiality;
d. obtain assistance from social workers and legal aid at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations; ande. obtain spiritual guidance services;
f. obtain protection from threats that endanger oneself, life and/or property whether before, during or after the case examination process from the Police;
g. obtain health rehabilitation, social rehabilitation, repatriation and social reintegration if the person concerned experiences suffering both physically and psychologically;
h. obtain restitution for victims of trafficking in persons or their heirs in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations; andi. other rights regulated in the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

35 -

Article 19

1. To implement the provisions as referred to in Article 18, Regional Government shall organize mainstreaming of children's rights including:a. establish the implementation of mainstreaming of children's rights;
b. coordinate and facilitate mainstreaming of children's rights;
c. facilitate institutional strengthening and development of child mainstreaming mechanisms in government institutions, research and development institutions, non-governmental institutions;
d. coordinate and facilitate child-responsive programs and activities and the development of communication, information, and education about mainstreaming of children's rights;

20 -
e. implement mainstreaming of children's rights related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;
f. improve the quality of life of children related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;
g. integrate development efforts related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;
h. coordinate the implementation of improving the quality of life of children related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;
i. endeavor to protect children especially victims of violence, children with disabilities in conflict areas and disaster-affected areas;
j. facilitate the integration of child protection especially protection for victims of violence, children with disabilities, in conflict areas and disaster-affected areas;
k. coordinate child protection especially protection for victims of violence, children with disabilities, in conflict areas and disaster-affected areas;
l. facilitate the strengthening and development of networks of community institutions or organizations and the business sector for the implementation of mainstreaming of children's rights; andm. provide and organize integrated or non-integrated services for child victims of violence, children with disabilities, and neglected children.

21 - (2) Further provisions regarding forms of child protection and mechanisms for mainstreaming children's rights as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

